* bench_trip: time-delay overcurrent element (behavioural). Ports a b; the element sits in series between them.
* Closed while its trip proxy is low, open once the proxy exceeds 1 (in units of IRATED).
* IRATED: trip current (A). IHOLD: current below which it closes again (A), below IRATED. IHOLD=0 never closes again (one-shot).
* TAU: time constant of the proxy (s). An overcurrent of I trips after TAU * ln(I / (I - IRATED)) seconds.
* RON / ROFF: closed and tripped resistance (ohm).
* The proxy is a 1 Mohm leak and a capacitor charged by a current-controlled source that copies the element current.
* Its initial charge is -1 mV: the element starts closed, and with IHOLD=0 it can never close again (the proxy only decays
* towards zero), which is the one-shot fuse.
* It is not a thermal model: no power, ambient temperature or latching (a real one-shot fuse stays open).
.subckt bench_trip a b params: IRATED=1 IHOLD=0.8 TAU=1 RON=1m ROFF=1G
VSENSE a m DC 0
STRIP m b ref bl tripsw
VREF ref 0 DC {1+(1+IHOLD/IRATED)/2}
RPROXY bl 0 1e6
CPROXY bl 0 {TAU/1e6} IC=-1m
FCHG 0 bl VSENSE {1/(IRATED*1e6)}
.model tripsw SW(VT=1 VH={(1-IHOLD/IRATED)/2} RON={RON} ROFF={ROFF})
.ends bench_trip
